Network:
     A computer network formed when two or more computers are connected in some way, to share information or resources more broadly. 
     A network is the interconnection of computing devices, from computers, to phones, to printers to smart devices and so on

Introduction to Data Communication
     Data communication refers to the exchange of data between two or more devices via some form of transmission medium 
     (such as a cable or wireless signal).
Fundamental Components of Data Communication
     Message: The information (data) to be communicated (text, numbers, pictures, audio, video).
     Sender: The device that sends the data message (computer, workstation, telephone handset, etc.).
     Receiver: The device that receives the message.
     Transmission Medium: The physical path by which a message travels from sender to receiver           
                         (twisted-pair wire, coaxial cable, fiber-optic cable, or radio waves).
     Protocol: A set of rules that govern data communication (e.g., TCP, IP, HTTP). It represents an agreement between the communicating devices.

Types of Computer Networks
           Networks are categorized based on their geographical scale and coverage area:
     PAN (Personal Area Network):
          Scope: Centered around an individual person within a single room or short range (typically within 10 meters).
          Example: Connecting a smartphone to a smartwatch or Bluetooth headphones.

     LAN (Local Area Network):
          Scope: Connects computers and devices within a limited geographical area, such as a home, office building, or school.
          Characteristics: High data transfer rates, secure, and privately owned.

     WLAN (Wireless Local Area Network):
          Scope: A LAN that relies on wireless communication technologies (like Wi-Fi) rather than physical cables.

     MAN (Metropolitan Area Network):
          Scope: Covers a larger geographic area than a LAN, typically spanning an entire city or town.
          Example: A city's cable television network or municipal Wi-Fi network.

     WAN (Wide Area Network):
          Scope: Spans a large geographical area, often a country, continent, or even the entire globe.
          Example: The Internet is the largest and most well-known WAN.

Network Topologies 
          Topology refers to the physical or logical arrangement of nodes and links in a network.
     Bus Topology: Devices are connected to a single central cable (the "bus").
          Pros: Easy to set up, uses less cable.
          Cons: If the main cable breaks, the entire network goes down.
      Star Topology: All devices are connected to a central hub or switch.
          Pros: Easy to troubleshoot; failure of one cable only disconnects that specific device.
          Cons: If the central hub fails, the whole network fails.
     Ring Topology: Devices are connected in a circular loop. Data travels in one direction.
          Pros: Equal access for all devices.
          Cons: A break in any single cable disrupts the entire ring.
     Mesh Topology: Every device is connected to every other device (Full Mesh) or to multiple devices (Partial Mesh).
          Pros: Highly redundant and fault-tolerant; extremely secure.
          Cons: Expensive and complex cabling.
     Tree Topology: A combination of Star and Bus topologies, arranged in a hierarchy (resembling branches on a tree).
     Hybrid Topology: A combination of two or more different topologies (e.g., Star-Ring).

