The OSI Model (Open Systems Interconnection)
          The OSI Model is a conceptual framework developed by the ISO that standardizes the functions of a telecommunication or computing system into
          7 distinct layers.
     1)Physical: Transmission and reception of raw bit streams over a physical medium.ex(Cables, Hubs, Repeater)
     2)Data Link: Physical addressing (MAC addresses), framing, and error detection on local links.ex(ethernet, Switches, MAC)
     3)Network: Logical addressing (IP addresses) and routing packets across networks.ex(IP,IPv4/IPv6, ICMP, Routers)
     4)Transport: End-to-end communication, flow control, and error correction.ex(TCP, UDP)
     5)Session: Establishes, manages, and terminates sessions between applications.ex(NetBIOS, RPC)
     6)Presentation: Data translation, encryption, compression, and formatting.ex(SSL/TLS, JPEG,)
     7)Application:	Network services directly to end-user applications (interface).ex(HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS)

The TCP/IP Model
          The TCP/IP Model is a more concise, 4-layer model that serves as the actual architectural foundation of the modern Internet.
     Layer 4: Application Layer
          Combines the functions of the OSI Application, Presentation, and Session layers.
          Handles high-level protocols, user representation, and session control. (Examples: HTTP, FTP, DNS)
     Layer 3: Transport Layer
          Equivalent to the OSI Transport layer. Provides reliable (TCP) or unreliable (UDP) delivery of data packets between hosts.
     Layer 2: Internet Layer
          Equivalent to the OSI Network layer. Handles packet routing, wrapping data into IP datagrams, and logical addressing
          . (Examples: IP, ICMP)
     Layer 1: Network Access (Link) Layer
          Combines OSI's Data Link and Physical layers. Deals with the physical hardware layout and local network media access.

OSI vs. TCP/IP Summary
     OSI is a theoretical reference model used for understanding and designing network architecture.
     TCP/IP is a practical, implementation-driven model built specifically for the Internet suite.
